return [====[nd Next, choose a chapter from the contents list, or search for a word such as &#x27;poison&#x27; or &#x27;Black Keycard&#x27;. Arrow keys turn chapters when you are not typing. The mouse wheel scrolls long pages. Text-size controls enlarge the print. Escape closes the menu.</p><p>The terminal TIME OVER sequence takes over the screen and input until its restart prompt. Reading does not stop danger, grant protection, or stop time.</p><blockquote>“One book. Two ways in. No excuse for getting lost.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 5</div></article><article class="page" id="staging" data-title="A moment with the Hermit"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A moment with the Hermit</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>Staging comes before Dungeon 1 and after each successful rescue. Speak to the Hermit, inspect your character in the mirror, check the Heroes of Legend board, and visit the Debbie statue for your wallet collection.</p><p>Your first Hermit visit issues one identity-bound advanced starter weapon; reopening the conversation does not issue another. Later staging visits offer one standard random-drop gift. Finish pending class and feat decisions, then choose your loadout.</p><p>Completing a dungeon resets the collapse clock to 30:00. It stays paused through the celebration and your return to staging, until the first Hero enters the next dungeon. Your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped gear, stored items, and consumable stacks stay with your Hero.</p><p>The portal commits you to the active dungeon. A Hero who remains behind cannot pause the countdown for teammates already inside.</p><p>Each rescued damsel stays in the staging room for the rest of the campaign. Approach her and press E for a short conversation and service. Restoratives refresh once per dungeon visit; equipment, the rescue fund grant, and the reserve life are once per Hero identity per campaign. Reconnecting does not renew a claim. Gifts you cannot use remain unclaimed.</p><p>The roster is Nessa, Bessa, Tessa, Odessa; Nell, Belle, Estelle, Mirabelle; May, Faye, Kay, Desiree; Jean, Colleen, Maureen, Josephine; Dora, Cora, Eleonora; and finally Deborah. Early families share complementary clothing and accents. The final four keep their natural appearances.</p><p>The first five replenish Pistol, SMG, Shotgun, .357 and AR2 ammunition respectively. Belle heals, Estelle cures ailments, and Mirabelle restores Magic. May through Maureen gift gloves, a ring, boots, headwear, a vest, trousers and a shield. Josephine supplies a Healing Potion; Dora restores full health; Cora grants 25–75 Debs; Eleonora restores one reserve life up to the normal maximum. Generated gear gifts cannot be resold or fused.</p><p>After Deborah’s rescue, Gordon announces his new vocation: stealing cash for the love of the game. Dungeons continue indefinitely with increasing encounter pressure and bounded enemy counts. The rescue roster resets with the campaign; persistent wallet records and Abundance cooldowns follow the existing server wallet rules.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 6</div></article><article class="page" id="clock" data-title="Thirty minutes. One campaign."><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Thirty minutes. One campaign.</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>Each dungeon has 1,800 seconds. Its clock starts when the first Hero commits to entering the labyrinth. A successful rescue resets the next dungeon clock to 30:00; it stays paused through staging until the first Hero enters.</p><p>Death, Tetris, menus, map use, staging visits, spectating, and disconnections do not pause or extend this clock. An empty server does not bank extra time for your return.</p><p>At zero, a seven-second prison demolition ends in GAME OVER: THE PRISON HAS COLLAPSED. A fresh campaign starts automatically 20 seconds after the collapse finishes. After five seconds of aftermath, PRESS E TO START A NEW GAME restarts sooner at Dungeon 1 with a fresh clock and roster.</p><p>A rescue completed earlier in the campaign does not exempt the party from the eventual timeout.</p><blockquote>“I appreciate the sightseeing. I would appreciate the rescue more.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 7</div></article><article class="page" id="route" data-title="Follow the keycards"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Follow the keycards</h2><table><tr><td>1 · Red</td><td>Find Red Keycard → open Red Gate</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Blue</td><td>Find Blue Keycard → open Blue Gate</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Yellow</td><td>Find Yellow Keycard → open Yellow Gate</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Black</td><td>Find Neil → defeat Neil → take Black Keycard</td></tr><tr><td>5 · The Warden</td><td>Open Black Gate → enter Gordon&#x27;s arena</td></tr><tr><td>6 · Rescue target</td><td>Defeat Gordon → take Jail Key → open chamber → rescue damsel / secure cash</td></tr></table><p>The labyrinth spans connected floors, stairs, passages, and gates. Its required route is solvable; a gate is a progression barrier, not a wall to bypass with a movement trick.</p><p>Watch the current objective and your floor. A target above or below you may require the stairs, not a turn into the nearest corridor. Defeating Brute alone never awards the Black Keycard.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 8</div></article><article class="page" id="map" data-title="Read the maze"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the maze</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 0%"></div><p>Press M for your map. It consumes Magic while open and suppresses Magic regeneration. At the base rate, a full 100 Magic lasts 15 seconds; Wisdom and applicable equipment or feats alter the cost. At zero Magic the map closes.</p><p>Use short looks to check your floor, route, and current objective. The map is information, not a pause screen. Your build determines available breadcrumbs and other navigation aids.</p><p>GPS requires its feat. Press G to toggle its private voice without spending Magic. When enabled, stand still for one second with no living enemies within two cells on your floor. It gives a direction, then can repeat after four seconds of silence. Movement, combat, or nearby enemies cancel the cue—even if those enemies are behind walls.</p><p>GPS follows the current legal objective route. It does not reveal loot, monsters, or future objectives. A server with visual maps disabled can still allow GPS.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 9</div></article><article class="page" id="character" data-title="Who are you this time?"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Who are you this time?</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>Your Hero has a generated name, appearance, Origin, Background, and Motive. Those three traits each grant a permanent perk: a favored weapon, a favored enemy, or ability points. Each chooses independently; matching perks can stack.</p><p>An ability perk gives either +2 to one ability or +1 to each of two different abilities. Read the actual trait on your Character Sheet to see what your Hero received.</p><p>Your starting scores use six rolls of four six-sided dice, dropping each lowest die. The initial array must total at least 74. A stored weakness pass then reduces the weakest score by 2d2 and the next two by 1d3 each. There is no second reroll after these reductions.</p><p>Death, reconnection, and dungeon transitions preserve your identity, class, growth, XP, and feats. A true campaign restart creates a fresh roster.</p><p>The Character Sheet groups meaningful final build statistics into Offense, Defense, Magic, Elemental, Status, Movement, Recovery and Summons / Utility. Inactive zero-value modifiers stay hidden.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 10</div></article><article class="page" id="abilities" data-title="Six scores, many surprises"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Six scores, many surprises</h2><table><tr><td>STR · Strength</td><td>Physical damage; Push; Fighter shield bonus</td></tr><tr><td>DEX · Dexterity</td><td>Movement (−10% to +55% from DEX); applicable attack handling; Clumsy and fire saves</td></tr><tr><td>CON · Constitution</td><td>HP growth, damage resistance, regeneration; poison and bleeding saves</td></tr><tr><td>INT · Intelligence</td><td>Magic regeneration and Arcane Shield integrity</td></tr><tr><td>WIS · Wisdom</td><td>Magic damage, approved range and utility scaling; Magic saves</td></tr><tr><td>CHA · Charisma</td><td>Morale and personality effects</td></tr></table><p>Scores stay within 3–30. An ability modifier is floor((score − 10) / 2): 10–11 gives +0, 12–13 gives +1, and 20–21 gives +5.</p><p>Equipment changes your active combat scores, but temporary gear does not qualify you for feats. Feat requirements use your permanent character abilities. The sheet distinguishes your build from what your equipped items contribute.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 11</div></article><article class="page" id="fighter" data-title="Fighter: stay in the fight"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Fighter: stay in the fight</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>A Fighter regenerates toward 33% of maximum HP from Level 1. After five continuous seconds without effective damage, regeneration starts at 1% of maximum HP per second, modified by Constitution. Damage restarts the delay. This does not refill temporary Tetris overfill.</p><p>Second Wind, Rapid Recovery, and Unbroken add 11%, 22%, or 33% to the ceiling; higher ranks replace lower ranks. A Fighter with these reaches 44%, 55%, or 66%. Other independent gear contributions can also help.</p><p>A Fighter protects half of a positive Strength damage modifier, rounded up, from Constitution reduction. The remaining part is reduced normally. This is a flat damage bonus, not a percentage bonus.</p><p>With a shield equipped, a Fighter also adds positive STR modifier percentage points to Block: STR +5 adds five points. All Block sources share one roll and a 33% cap. No shield means no class Block bonus.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 12</div></article><article class="page" id="rogue" data-title="Rogue: keep moving"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Rogue: keep moving</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 100%"></div><p>Rogues gain 11% ordinary voluntary movement speed in every direction, or 22% total while sprinting. The class itself adds no jump-height bonus.</p><p>Rogue Dodge contributes 11 percentage points ordinarily and 22 at the fast movement tier. Dodge needs actual voluntary speed: below one quarter of walking speed it is zero. Below 90% of sprint speed, the combined cap is 33%; at or above it, the cap is 66%. Forced movement and jumping do not manufacture extra Dodge. Magic cannot be Dodged.</p><p>Rogue damage dice have better explosion thresholds: d6 continues on 5+, d12 on 7+, and other eligible dice on their maximum. Sealed rolls such as generation or healing are not damage explosions.</p><p>A direct attack from behind Backstabs: it ignores all Constitution damage reduction and adds one damage multiple. Normal ×1 becomes ×2; aimed ×2 becomes ×3; Deadeye ×3 becomes ×4. Passive and status damage do not Backstab.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 13</div></article><article class="page" id="wizard" data-title="Wizard: spend wisely"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Wizard: spend wisely</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>A Wizard receives one extra distinct Content at Level 1 and is the only class that can acquire and cast SUMMON. All classes can use their other owned Forms and Contents.</p><p>Arcane Shield automatically diverts part of incoming resolved HP damage into Magic. It begins at 10%, adds 2.5 percentage points per Level, and stops at 50%. Intended diverted HP rounds upward. Normally one Magic pays for one diverted HP; unpaid damage still reaches HP.</p><p>Magical damage can test Arcane Integrity and shatter the shield. Physical hits can trigger Feedback under its class rules. Arcane Shattered disables the shield until recovery. These are automatic defenses, not voluntary spell casts.</p><p>Magic capacity is always 100. INT 10 regenerates at the base rate; INT 20 at about 1.71 times that rate; INT 30 at three times the rate. Low INT never falls below 85% of base regeneration, but an open map suppresses that regeneration first. Living Aegis improves shield efficiency, not pool size. Non-Wizards can instead obtain the Mana Barrier feat family; Wizards cannot draft it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 14</div></article><article class="page" id="wizard-offense" data-title="A Wizard’s bright ideas"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A Wizard’s bright ideas</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>Arcane Surge rewards a full Magic pool. An eligible attack committed at full Magic receives a bonus equal to your positive Intelligence modifier. Its commitment snapshot matters: spending the spell’s cost does not retroactively erase an earned full-pool bonus.</p><p>Feedback is a reactive counterattack. When an eligible hit deals HP damage to a Wizard whose shield is online, Feedback’s chance equals your current diversion fraction. A successful response has a one-second cooldown. It rolls one d4 for each actual die in the incoming damage contract, including continuations, then adds positive INT modifier.</p><p>Feedback is Magic damage, but does not add Wisdom or recursively trigger Feedback. An Arcane Shattered shield cannot produce this response. The separate Feedback Loop feat restores Magic from eligible damage-die continuations; read its entry for the per-cast cap.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 15</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-defenses" data-title="Read the resistance"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the resistance</h2><p>Monsters and controlled Soldiers have lighter defenses than Heroes: Constitution removes at most 1 HP per damage die, and Arcane Shield absorbs at most 30% without rounding small hits away. Hero Constitution, shield and Feedback rules stay as described in their class entries.</p><p>Enemy Feedback has at most a 15% chance and a two-second cooldown. Magical hits have an easier shield-break check; a successful break disables diversion and Feedback for at least 24 seconds, or 1.5 times the rolled duration if longer. A break or defeat also cancels a still-pending retaliation.</p><p>Read the combat feed for the reason and a useful response. Backstabs bypass Constitution; larger hits lose less to its per-die reduction. Magic can break Arcane Shield, while Poison bypasses diversion. True Faith resists Magic, so try physical attacks. Mind Over Matter resists a physical hit, then leaves a recovery window for your follow-up.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 16</div></article><article class="page" id="leveling" data-title="Grow into a legend"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Grow into a legend</h2><p>Heroes begin at Combat Level 1 with 100 base HP. Later Levels use a stored class hit die: Fighter d10, Rogue d8, Wizard d4. Constitution&#x27;s per-Level HP bonus is capped at +6. Reopening the sheet or reconnecting does not reroll growth.</p><p>The current Hero Level ceiling is the lower of 20 and Dungeon Level +3. XP earned at the temporary ceiling is banked until the ceiling rises. Level 20 uses 96,000 total XP, after which Hero XP stops.</p><p>A kill awards 40% of its XP to the finisher and shares 60% by HP damage contribution. Defeating a human Soldier does not yield Hero XP. Each deployed Hero receives rescue XP of 500 + 100 × min(Dungeon Level, 20).</p><p>Choose one of three stored feat offers at Levels 1, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, and 18. Pending choices do not reroll on reopening, death, or reconnection. Level 20 adds one class capstone choice. There are no extra Rogue feat drafts at Levels 2, 5, 10, or 15.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 17</div></article><article class="page" id="feats" data-title="Choose your specialty"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Choose your specialty</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>A feat may require permanent ability scores, a previous rank, a weapon or Magic capability, or a particular actor type. The draft filters these requirements before presenting three distinct offers. Choose one; the other two are not yours.</p><p>Ranked families usually replace their own lower rank. Separate sources add only where their rules say they do. Six repeatable fallback feats can raise individual permanent abilities when offered, up to the intrinsic score ceiling.</p><p>The reference chapters at the back include every current ordinary feat, all six fallbacks, and the nine class capstones. Their requirements and effect descriptions come from the same catalog used by the game.</p><p>Wall Jump grants four successful kicks before landing, each requiring a fresh Jump press near a static vertical wall. Holding Jump and touching a wall do not refill them. Cloud Step is separate: spend 3 Magic once before landing for a double-impulse extra jump. Hold a direction for a modest horizontal boost. Wall Jump takes priority when a legal kick is available. These jumps retain their full strength; invisible walls above the crates block maze shortcuts while leaving legal upper-floor paths and stairs open.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 18</div></article><article class="page" id="combat" data-title="Every hit tells a story"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Every hit tells a story</h2><p>A damage expression such as 2d6 means two six-sided dice. Eligible damage dice can explode: a qualifying result adds another die, which may continue the chain. Generation, progression, healing, and other sealed rolls do not inherit damage explosions.</p><p>Strength adds once to a physical damage contract per target; Wisdom does the same for Wisdom-scaled Magic. Constitution reduces positive die contributions, with the ordinary minimum-one rule. Strength and Wisdom are flat additions, not percentage multipliers.</p><p>Aimed and Backstab multiples, equipment, elements, Dodge, Block, and Arcane Shield then matter according to their rules. A zero or immune attack does not become damaging merely because a bonus exists.</p><p>Watch the lower-right readout, or press L for the Die Log. Both display the same authoritative events: rolls, saves, damage, Dodge, status effects, regeneration, Magic, Push, XP, and more. A DODGE can be recorded even when damage is zero. Names and labels carry meaning as well as color.</p><p>Nearby player rolls appear in your Die Logger. Wisdom expands your listening range. Look directly at a teammate to see their username as their character, in the same semantic colors. Active ailments replace the HUD character name, color the portrait and trigger an onset cue; afflicted enemies show matching effects and status events.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 19</div></article><article class="page" id="weapons" data-title="Know your weapons"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Know your weapons</h2><table><tr><td>Crowbar</td><td>Base 1d3; no ammunition. Bash and Walloper improve it.</td></tr><tr><td>Pistol</td><td>1d4; a straightforward sidearm.</td></tr><tr><td>Shotgun</td><td>1d6 with a minimum contribution of 3 per damage die; shared shell damage and strong hit stun.</td></tr><tr><td>SMG</td><td>1d8; rapid fire builds heat.</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse Rifle / AR2</td><td>1d10 projectiles; one cartridge powers a three-shot burst.</td></tr><tr><td>Revolver / .357</td><td>1d12 with special exploding chains and penetrating shots.</td></tr></table><p>Dice are the starting point, not guaranteed final damage. Your abilities, class, feats, aimed hits, range, armor, and elemental matchup can change the outcome.</p><p>Right mouse casts your chosen spell while a normal weapon is held. It does not use the stock game&#x27;s grenade launcher or alternate-fire attack. Ordinary explosive grenades are not part of this equipment economy.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 20</div></article><article class="page" id="weapon-tricks" data-title="Fire with a little finesse"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Fire with a little finesse</h2><p>SMG heat rises as you fire. Its baseline heat threshold is six; heat cools in quarter-second steps, and overheating locks it for two seconds. Watch and listen for warm, near-overheat, and ready feedback. Heat-control feats change the weapon&#x27;s limits.</p><p>The Pulse Rifle has a 0.45-second tell before a three-projectile burst, with 0.09-second spacing and 0.25-second recovery. The whole baseline burst spends one cartridge. Burst-size feats add projectiles through this same attack.</p><p>The Revolver uses a special d12 continuation ladder: the fresh die explodes at 8+, then later thresholds descend through 7, 6, and 5 in its ordinary chain. Rogue and relevant feats change eligible thresholds. Its magazine progress builds bonuses, and its later shots can fire extra projectiles. It also has a baseline 25% chance to conserve a cartridge.</p><p>The Shotgun rolls 8 plus an independent exploding d6 for pellet count, capped at 36. Shell damage is divided into six shares and applied according to each target’s pellet hits, with the normal minimum-one rule. Its hit stun is four times the ordinary duration. The pellet-count roll is utility, not damage, so damage feats do not inflate it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 21</div></article><article class="page" id="aim-and-cylinder" data-title="Hold steady. Then fire."><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hold steady. Then fire.</h2><p>The Revolver can enter an aimed state after you keep position and aim still for a base 0.5 seconds. An aimed shot deals ×2. Moving, changing your aim, switching weapons, or attacking breaks or consumes the state. Faster-aim effects reduce the required hold.</p><p>Deadeye makes the Revolver’s aimed multiple ×3 and lets the other supported ordinary weapons aim for ×2. A Rogue’s qualifying Backstab then adds one more multiple. Holding right mouse is not the aiming command; right mouse is your Magic control.</p><p>The Revolver’s cylinder adds a flat bonus equal to chambers already spent: ordinarily +0 through +5. Its fifth cartridge fires two projectiles; its sixth fires three. Below 60 HP, each HP below 60 adds one percentage point of chance for one extra projectile. Below 34% maximum HP, each full percentage point below 34 adds one point of chance to preserve the final cartridge. These conditional chances are separate from its baseline 25% ammunition conservation.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 22</div></article><article class="page" id="ammo" data-title="Make every round count"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make every round count</h2><table><tr><td>Weapon</td><td>Magazine / total carried / base recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Pistol</td><td>18 / 54 / 60 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Shotgun</td><td>7 / 21 / 90 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>SMG</td><td>25 / 75 / 120 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse Rifle</td><td>20 / 60 / 150 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Revolver</td><td>6 / 18 / 180 seconds</td></tr></table><p>Total carried includes the loaded magazine. All copies of one weapon family share ammunition; collecting another copy does not create an independent ammo reserve.</p><p>Passive recovery prevents complete deadlock. Below the normal one-magazine floor, ammunition returns gradually after an interruption delay. The times above are the base empty-to-floor recovery schedules; firing and applicable feats affect recovery. It is not a full-ammo refill.</p><p>Ammo pickups favor an owned family with room. Reload deliberately, use the crowbar when appropriate, and save powerful shots for threats that deserve them.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 23</div></article><article class="page" id="magic" data-title="Put a spell together"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Put a spell together</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>Open I for the Spellbook. Choose an owned Form to decide how the spell travels, then an owned Content to decide its element and rider. Cast with the Form’s bound mouse button (RMB by default). The Form&#x27;s base cost and the Content&#x27;s surcharge combine before applicable cost rules.</p><p>Every Hero begins with one random direct-damage Form and RAW Content. Wall and Summon never replace that starting spell; Wizards may learn them through later grants. Wizards receive an additional distinct Content at Level 1. The normal Content grants occur at Levels 4, 8, and 14. Only a Wizard can acquire or cast SUMMON or WALL.</p><p>Magic tops out at 100. Intelligence governs regeneration; Wisdom governs spell power and approved range, radius, and utility scaling. A spell&#x27;s visible area boundary marks its actual area, while a Beam ends where world geometry stops it.</p><p>When holding any throwable, its controls take priority. Switch back to a normal weapon to cast.</p><p>Spellbook labels distinguish READY / AVAILABLE, LOCKED, NEED MAGIC, COOLDOWN, and BLOCKED. Solid floors stop all spell areas. Open stairwells and shafts still allow exposed vertical hits.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 24</div></article><article class="page" id="forms" data-title="Ten Forms of Magic"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Ten Forms of Magic</h2><table><tr><td>CONE · 22</td><td>3d6; immediate forward force cone, 64° wide. WIS scales reach; Content supplies its element and rider.</td></tr><tr><td>BLAST · 30</td><td>2d6; connected visible cells around the caster. Solid walls and floors block damage.</td></tr><tr><td>BEAM · 18</td><td>3d6; piercing line, 0.65-second cast recovery. Stops at solid geometry.</td></tr><tr><td>BOMB · 20</td><td>3d6; lobbed area attack. Throw range fixed; radius scales with WIS.</td></tr><tr><td>MISSILE · 28</td><td>3d6; steerable projectile with area impact. WIS affects range and radius.</td></tr><tr><td>BOLT · 15</td><td>4d6; fast precision shot. No area or body piercing.</td></tr><tr><td>SUPER BALL · 32</td><td>2d6 per contact; a chaotic bouncing ball can strike several enemies or return to the same one. Six-second lifetime, up to six hits, at most two active per caster. Walls and floors redirect it.</td></tr><tr><td>WATERMELON · 24</td><td>Roll 1d6 bounces on throw. Enemy contacts deal 1d6 with a brief per-target cooldown; the last bounce shatters for 2d6 area damage. Lower base blast radius than Bomb, with WIS radius scaling. Expires after eight seconds; clear rind/flesh effects mark the shatter.</td></tr><tr><td>SUMMON · 12</td><td>Wizard only: call a gold Seeker for 20 seconds. Default cap: one.</td></tr><tr><td>WALL · 35</td><td>Wizard only. A barrier up to 288 units wide, 112 high and 12 thick fits nearby clear space; WIS increases its 10-second duration by two seconds per positive modifier, up to 30 seconds. Heroes pass through; enemies are blocked and take 2d6 contact damage. The server preview shows the actual fitted shape and placement reason. Only one active Wall per caster. Hold its bound mouse button to see the aiming guide; release to place. Opening a menu cancels the pending placement.</td></tr></table><p>Numbers beside names are base Magic costs before Content. A summoned Seeker carries the selected Content into its attacks. An invalid placement that cannot find safe ground and hull clearance fails without spending Magic.</p><p>Beam can hit each eligible body along its resolved line once. Its blue/cyan core remains recognizable even with an elemental accent.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 25</div></article><article class="page" id="contents" data-title="Give your spell a character"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Give your spell a character</h2><table><tr><td>RAW · +0</td><td>Untyped Magic; no Content rider</td></tr><tr><td>EARTH · +10</td><td>336-unit Push attempt</td></tr><tr><td>FIRE · +15</td><td>Immolated attempt</td></tr><tr><td>DARK · +15</td><td>Poisoned attempt</td></tr><tr><td>ICE · +10</td><td>Held attempt; Wisdom save</td></tr><tr><td>LIGHT · +5</td><td>Muted attempt</td></tr><tr><td>ELECTRIC · +10</td><td>Morale / Intimidated attempt</td></tr></table><p>A rider is an attempt, not a guaranteed condition. The attack must deal qualifying damage to a surviving target; then the appropriate save or Morale rules apply. A duplicate guaranteed rider does not secretly create repeated rolls for the same event.</p><p>Contents are class-neutral. A Fighter or Rogue may attach any Content they own to a Form they can use. Summoning itself remains Wizard-only.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 26</div></article><article class="page" id="elements" data-title="Find their weak side"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Find their weak side</h2><table><tr><td>Earth ↔ Electric</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr><tr><td>Fire ↔ Ice</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr><tr><td>Dark ↔ Light</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr></table><p>Each generated monster independently has a one-in-three chance of elemental affinity. If it has one, the six elements are equally likely. An Earth creature resists Earth and is weak to Electric; the same reciprocal rule applies to each pair.</p><p>The other four elements and untyped damage remain neutral unless another explicit effect says otherwise. Weakness and resistance use their own single-roll ladders, from 11% through 88%; do not assume several visible sources mean several separate reduction rolls.</p><p>An elemental aura and aimed elemental words help identify affinity. Class tint and elemental affinity are different clues: a Rogue or Wizard can have any element. Gear may add elemental damage, wards, or a weakness. Read both the benefit and the drawback.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 27</div></article><article class="page" id="conditions" data-title="Trouble you can shake off"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Trouble you can shake off</h2><table><tr><td>Clumsy · DEX</td><td>Slower movement and impaired attack handling; 1+1d4 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Immolated · DEX</td><td>Burning damage; recovery checks can extinguish it.</td></tr><tr><td>Poisoned · CON</td><td>Loses HP on cell movement; periodic saves can clear it.</td></tr><tr><td>Bleeding · CON</td><td>Repeated damage ticks with recovery saves.</td></tr><tr><td>Muted · WIS</td><td>Prevents voluntary Magic casting; 1+1d4 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Held · WIS</td><td>Stops voluntary movement; explosive 1d6 duration.</td></tr><tr><td>Reckless · WIS</td><td>Disrupts restraint; AI can turn on nearby allies.</td></tr><tr><td>Arcane Shattered · INT</td><td>Arcane Shield is offline until recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Intimidated · CHA</td><td>Brief player disruption from failed Morale.</td></tr></table><p>The listed ability is the relevant condition-save lane. Many durations are rolled, so repeated applications need not last equally long. Status riders and feat procs still respect their saves. Reapplication follows the condition&#x27;s own refresh rule, rather than creating unlimited copies.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 28</div></article><article class="page" id="defenses" data-title="Dodge, Block, Push, Morale"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dodge, Block, Push, Morale</h2><p>Dodge combines eligible sources into one speed-gated roll against physical attacks. Block combines eligible sources into another shared physical defense, capped at 33%. Neither is a promise of invulnerability. Wizard diversion spends Magic after ordinary mitigation.</p><p>Push is a resolved attack effect, not free movement. Strength, size, equipment, and relevant feats affect its attempt and distance. A legitimate wall collision can become a wall slam; ordinary contact with scenery is not automatically bonus damage.</p><p>Hit stun can interrupt a hostile&#x27;s preparation. Watch telegraphs: canceling a charge is different from trying to stop an attack that has already been released.</p><p>Morale compares the attacker&#x27;s Charisma pressure with the defender&#x27;s save when a qualifying trauma or effect calls for it. A failed check can make an AI flee; a player receives the authored brief Intimidated response instead of losing the controls to AI. Cooldowns prevent every pellet from demanding another Morale check.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 29</div></article><article class="page" id="equipment" data-title="Dress for the rescue"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dress for the rescue</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>Equipment has a bag and dedicated slots. You can keep multiple copies of a weapon family and select the one to equip. Touching a pickup stores the item; it does not silently replace your carefully chosen gear. Procedural pistols and crowbars drop from Dungeon 1 alongside the other early weapons, even if you already own the starter versions.</p><p>Wearable families are headwear, vest, trousers, boots, rings, gloves, and shields. A ring can use either hand. A pair of gloves occupies both hands; displacing it frees both. The shield uses the left-arm accessory slot, not a ring slot.</p><p>Your ordinary equipment storage holds 32 records, including equipped gear. The inventory shows only the actual free equipment slots and an exact used/total counter. Consumables appear below the gear grid with their separate per-type stack limits. Only equipped wearables and the currently wielded weapon contribute their effects. A powerful weapon sitting in the bag does nothing to your current attack.</p><p>Drag equipment onto a highlighted valid slot to equip; drag it into the bag to stow. Drop unwanted, unequipped equipment onto the visible trash can to destroy it immediately with a disposal sound. Protected progression objects cannot be trashed. The server confirms the final position after every action.</p><p>Weapon identity uses a soft aura, distinct muzzle behavior, and two independently selected tints while preserving a canonical grip or stock. Each stock weapon has three physical regions while wielded, in a teammate’s hands and as a pickup, including models with a single native material. Tint A follows the element; Tint B follows a mechanical trait family, with shades derived from the individual roll. Surface patterns are retired. Wintery means Ice, including Wintery Warding and Wintery Frailty.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 30</div></article><article class="page" id="loot" data-title="Read the fine print"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the fine print</h2><table><tr><td>Unusual · 65%</td><td>4 benefits; value factor 1.00</td></tr><tr><td>Rare · 25%</td><td>5 benefits; value factor 1.20</td></tr><tr><td>Exalted · 9%</td><td>6 benefits; value factor 1.45</td></tr><tr><td>Legendary · 1%</td><td>7 benefits; value factor 1.75</td></tr></table><p>Current generated gear also has one drawback and one elemental damage identity. Weapons always carry a hit rider. An item&#x27;s effect depends on its actual rolls; a grand name alone is no guarantee it suits your build.</p><p>Equipment power scales with Dungeon Level up to 999, even after Hero Level stops at 20. Quality and family affect its budget; paired gloves account for their two occupied slots. Value is a comparison score, not cash in hand.</p><p>Properties can affect abilities, saves, damage, speed, Dodge, Block, regeneration, Push, map cost, breadcrumbs, summons, elemental wards, weaknesses, and hit riders. Conditional damage bonuses say when they work: injured, almost stationary, or highly charged with Magic. The equipment-property reference lists every current property.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 31</div></article><article class="page" id="supplies" data-title="Supplies in the corridors"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Supplies in the corridors</h2><p>Ordinary drops are character-owned. A pickup you can inspect is not necessarily an item another player can claim. Walk into your eligible pickup to collect it. A full bag or full consumable stack leaves an unaccepted pickup available rather than silently destroying it.</p><p>Early dungeons offer more need-aware assistance: resource shortages and long dry spells can influence useful drops. This help decreases through Dungeons 2–10; from Dungeon 11 onward the assistance layer no longer provides pity, objective guarantees, deficit steering, or resource-budget supplements. Base random loot remains.</p><p>Normal ammo pickups add up to 6 Pistol rounds, 3 Shotgun shells, 9 SMG rounds, 6 Pulse Rifle rounds, or 2 Revolver rounds, limited by room. The carried caps still include the magazine. Equipment drops, consumables, and temporary ammunition are ordinary run inventory; they are not persistent DFTs.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 32</div></article><article class="page" id="bottles" data-title="A bottle for a friend"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A bottle for a friend</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>Healing Potions, Stink Bombs, Summon Cards and Feathers of Resurrection each stack up to three. Equip a stack in the Throwable slot, then select it as your held weapon. Its controls appear on screen.</p><p>Healing Potion: right mouse drinks it to restore up to 25 HP. At full ordinary HP, drinking is rejected without consuming one. Left mouse throws it; the first living allied Hero hit receives the healing. There is no healing splash. A missed throw still spends the bottle.</p><p>Stink Bomb: left mouse throws a five-second poison cloud with a 96-unit radius. Enemies entering make the normal Constitution save against Poisoned. You cannot drink a Stink Bomb.</p><p>Summon Card: choose another deployed Hero. Left mouse brings them to a safe adjacent square; right mouse brings them to your square or its nearest safe equivalent. Closed gates still apply. A cancelled or failed selection spends nothing.</p><p>Feather of Resurrection: either mouse button restores the oldest eligible zero-life Hero in the Hero Queue with exactly one life, preserving their character and surviving inventory. Active Soldiers and Soldier respawn waits are excluded; returning to the Hero Queue restores eligibility. Full Hero slots defer admission. No eligible Hero means no Feather spent.</p><p>These bottles, cards and Feathers spend no Magic. Holding one replaces spell controls; switch weapons before trying to cast a spell.</p><p>Healing Potions are remedies: they also clear every active negative condition, even at full HP. Magic bombs cover Physical, RAW, all six elements and every condition. They share normal throwable slots and saves; their 144-unit explosion cannot hit through solid walls or floors.</p><blockquote>“The red one is medicine. Please read the label.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 33</div></article><article class="page" id="moves" data-title="Equipment with a trick"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Equipment with a trick</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 100%"></div><table><tr><td>Quickstep · boots</td><td>↑ ↑ ↑ · 10 Magic · 2-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Rebuff · ring</td><td>← ↓ → · 20 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Psychic Crush · Crown of Psychic Crushing</td><td>↓ ↑ ↓ · 18 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>Quickstep dashes forward up to 90 units over 0.2 seconds; walls stop it. Ordinary voluntary-speed Dodge rules apply. It is not permission to cross a locked gate.</p><p>Rebuff sends a one-cell burst of 1d6 untyped Magic, followed by a 96-unit Push attempt against surviving damaged enemies.</p><p>Psychic Crush targets the nearest visible enemy within two connected maze cells. Closed gates and solid cover block it. It deals 2d8 plus your Wisdom bonus as Magic; the enemy makes a Wisdom save for half damage. The die readout identifies the target and save. With no valid target, you spend no Magic. The Crown is at least Rare and its innate power is included in its value.</p><p>You must equip an item that grants the move. Enter its sequence with the arrow keys. The default alternate keys map Up to [, Down to comma, Left to semicolon, and Right to backslash. Rebind those mirror keys in Equipment if needed.</p><p>Pause more than 0.8 seconds between inputs and the sequence resets. An unavailable move, insufficient Magic, or an active cooldown cannot be bypassed by entering the combination faster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 34</div></article><article class="page" id="fighting-streets" data-title="Gloves of the Fighting Streets"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Gloves of the Fighting Streets</h2><table><tr><td>Ember Fist</td><td>← ↓ → · 12 Magic · 2-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Cinder Rise</td><td>→ ↓ → · 18 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>These paired gloves occupy both hands and grant both techniques while equipped. They are at least Rare; their affixes and innate techniques contribute to their value. Stowing them removes the techniques.</p><p>Ember Fist launches a straight elemental fireball up to five cells (1920 units), dealing 2d6 plus Wisdom as Magic to the first enemy hit. Solid cover stops it; there is no splash or piercing. The gloves’ affinity determines the element and its ordinary Content rider: Earth Push, Fire Immolated, Dark Poisoned, Ice Held, Light Muted or Electric Morale.</p><p>Cinder Rise strikes visible enemies in your current maze square for 2d8 plus Wisdom as Magic, using the gloves’ element. Surviving damaged enemies attempt the ordinary Immolated save. Its upward effect does not move your Hero; walls and floors block its hits.</p><p>Both use ordinary offensive Magic cost modifiers, without a Content surcharge. A committed miss still costs Magic. An invalid source, blocked launch, insufficient Magic or cooldown spends nothing. Stale fireballs disappear if you die, change roles or dungeons, or remove the source gloves; they do not refund Magic.</p><p>Rebuff uses the same sequence as Ember Fist, but its ring cannot stay equipped alongside these two-hand gloves. Read your equipped item’s details before entering the sequence.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 35</div></article><article class="page" id="invisibility-ring" data-title="Ring of Invisibility"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Ring of Invisibility</h2><table><tr><td>Veil</td><td>← ↑ ← · 20 Magic · 20-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>This single-hand ring grants up to 12 seconds of Invisibility while equipped. It is at least Rare and can occupy either hand. Paired gloves displace it. Its generated properties and innate technique both contribute to its value.</p><p>AI enemies lose you as a target while invisible. Their shots already in flight, committed area attacks, environmental hazards, and manually aimed human Soldiers can still hurt you. Sixth Sense can reveal your silhouette under its ordinary visibility rules.</p><p>Primary or secondary attack input reveals you, even if the attempt is dry or blocked. Magic on any bound button, an offensive item technique, a throw attempt, or dealing or taking HP damage also reveals you. Walking, jumping, reloading, opening menus and drinking a potion alone preserve Veil.</p><p>Removing or moving the source ring, death, a role or dungeon change, and other lifecycle transitions end Veil. It cannot refresh while active. Ending never refunds Magic or resets the cooldown. Failed activation spends nothing; Veil costs a fixed 20 Magic.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 36</div></article><article class="page" id="thunder-hat" data-title="Hat of the Thunder God"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hat of the Thunder God</h2><table><tr><td>Thunder Charge</td><td>↑ ↓ ↑ · 24 base Magic · 6-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>This Rare-or-better headwear grants Thunder Charge while equipped. Its generated properties and innate technique both contribute to its value. Stowing or replacing the Hat ends an active charge.</p><p>An electric-blue path and sound warn for a quarter-second, then you charge straight along your original aim for up to three blocks in 0.8 seconds. You cannot steer. Walls, locked gates, actors, stairs, ledges, wet or harmful spaces stop the charge. Start on a supported flat floor with at least 32 units of safe room.</p><p>The first enemy contact deals one hit of 2d8 plus Wisdom as electric Magic, with its normal Morale rider. Surviving enemies that lose HP may suffer ordinary hit-stun, subject to their existing immunities and retrigger rules. Allies stop your charge without taking damage. There is no piercing or splash.</p><p>The charge grants no invulnerability, flight or extra Dodge. It uses ordinary offensive Magic cost modifiers and reveals Veil. Failed activation is free; once committed, interruption or a miss does not refund Magic or reset the cooldown. Removal, death, role or dungeon changes, forced movement, Held or Muted cancel it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 37</div></article><article class="page" id="heavy-plumber" data-title="Boots of the Heavy Plumber"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Heavy Plumber</h2><table><tr><td>Heavy Stomp</td><td>Land from above · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>These Rare-or-better boots occupy your feet slot. Equip them before leaving solid ground, then land on an enemy from above to deal 2d6 plus Strength as physical damage. Your usual dice, defenses and damage modifiers apply. Sideways contact and landing on allies do not attack.</p><p>A valid stomp gives you a short upward rebound, even if the enemy dodges or blocks the damage. Low ceilings reduce the rebound. With insufficient clearance or unsafe floor support below, you land normally without an attack. The Boots do not grant flight, invulnerability or protection from fall damage.</p><p>You get one stomp attempt per airborne excursion, with at least 0.65 seconds between commitments. Your bounce is part of that same excursion. Land on solid, non-actor support to rearm; standing on an enemy or swapping Boots in midair will not do it.</p><p>Heavy Stomp needs no arrow sequence or Magic. Muted and holding a potion do not disable it; Held does. A committed stomp reveals Veil. Removing the Boots or changing life, role or dungeon invalidates the armed landing.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 38</div></article><article class="page" id="death" data-title="Down, but not finished"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Down, but not finished</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background]====]
